      "marks": 4,
      "chapter": "Electrochemistry",
      "topic": "Galvanic cells and electrode potential",
      "question": "For the Daniell cell, $E^\\circ_{\\text{Cu}^{2+}/\\text{Cu}} = +0.34\\,\\text{V}$ and $E^\\circ_{\\text{Zn}^{2+}/\\text{Zn}} = -0.76\\,\\text{V}$. The standard cell potential $E^\\circ_{cell}$ is:",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$1.10\\ \\text{V}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$0.42\\ \\text{V}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$-1.10\\ \\text{V}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$0.76\\ \\text{V}$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "A",
      "solution": {
        "finalAnswer": "$1.10\\ \\text{V}$",
        "stepByStep": [
          "Cu has the higher reduction potential, so it is the cathode; Zn is the anode.",
          "$E^\\circ_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cathode} - E^\\circ_{anode}$.",
          "$E^\\circ_{cell} = 0.34 - (-0.76) = 1.10\\ \\text{V}$."
        ],
        "formulaUsed": [
          "E_cell = E_cathode - E_anode"
        ],
        "commonMistake": "Subtracting in the wrong order or dropping the sign of the anode potential.",
        "examinerNote": "A positive $E^\\circ_{cell}$ confirms a spontaneous (galvanic) cell."
      },
      "qualityChecks": {
